# Puerto Ink Academy — Cursor Rules

## Tech Stack
- Next.js 15 (App Router, Server Actions, TypeScript strict). Do not use `any`.
- Styling: Tailwind CSS and shadcn/ui. Dark theme only, mobile-first, premium gaming look.
- Database: PostgreSQL via Drizzle ORM. The live schema is `db/schema.sql`. The typed map is `src/db/schema.ts`.
- Storage: DigitalOcean Spaces (S3-compatible) plus its CDN. Postgres stores the CDN URL and the object key, never the file bytes.
- Roles: `ADMIN`, `TEACHER`, `APPRENTICE`.
- Auth is not built yet. `src/lib/auth/current-user.ts` is the temporary stand-in. Do not invent sessions, passwords, or login flows unless asked.

## Core Architecture
- Default to React Server Components.
- Add `"use client"` only when the screen needs interaction: forms, dialogs, tabs, or local claim state.
- Fetch in server components with the functions in `src/db/queries.ts`.
- Do not import `src/db` from a client component. The database module is `server-only`.
- New screens read Postgres. Do not add new mock data. `src/lib/mock/` stays only for screens that are not on the database yet.

## UI and UX
- Dark gaming aesthetic only. Amber for apprentices and teachers, violet for admins, emerald for a pass, rose for a fail, gold for a 10/10.
- Wrap each data section in `<Suspense>` with a skeleton from `src/components/feedback/`.
- The skeleton matches the layout the user will see once data arrives. It does not mimic the empty message.
- An empty query returns an empty state. It must not throw or render a broken list.
- Keep components modular and responsive.

## Business Logic
- A submission passes at 8/10 or higher and unlocks the next level. Under 8 stays on that level and can be retried.
- XP is written when the grade is saved: 10 awards 100, 9 awards 90, 8 awards 80, under 8 awards 0. Retries do not stack. The level keeps the best attempt.
- One pending submission per apprentice per level. A waiting submission blocks another upload.
- A best score of 10 closes the level. A best score of 8 or 9 unlocks the next level and still allows another attempt.
- The curriculum is shared. Teachers create a level once. Every apprentice uses that same level.
- Editing a lesson inserts a new `level_versions` row. Never update or delete an old version. Submissions stay pinned to the version that was graded.
- Level position does not change after the level exists. New levels are added at the end.
- Leaderboards are per style, read from stored `style_progress` XP. Rank is numbered when the page is requested. There is no leaderboard table and no global board.
- Personal level is stored on `apprentice_progress` from personal XP. Rank titles, when they exist, are looked up from `titles` by that level. They are not copied onto the apprentice.
- A badge is a level whose best score is 10. First versus best is derived from attempt history.

## Commit Conventions
Use Conventional Commits. The subject says why, in one line.

- `feat:` a new capability
- `fix:` a bug
- `refactor:` a restructure that keeps behavior
- `chore:` tooling, config, or dependencies

Do not commit `.env` or `.env.local`.
